    concept: "General Aptitude - Verbal Aptitude (Modal Auxiliaries)",
    markdown: "- **Problem Statement:**\n  *\"I have not yet decided what I will do this evening; I ______ visit a friend.\"*\n\n- **Grammatical Principle & Modal Auxiliaries:**\n  - Modal auxiliary verbs express degrees of possibility, intention, certainty, or obligation.\n  - The preceding independent clause, *\"I have not yet decided what I will do this evening\"*, explicitly establishes an uncertain, tentative future possibility rather than a firm, pre-planned decision or habitual action.\n\n- **Option & Distractor Analysis:**\n  - **Option A (\"mite\"):** A noun denoting a minute arachnid or a tiny amount; a phonetic homophone and grammatically invalid.\n  - **Option B (\"would\"):** Expresses definite conditional intent or past habit, conflicting with the undecided premise.\n  - **Option C (\"might\"):** Correct modal auxiliary indicating a tentative possibility or likelihood (*it is possible that I will visit a friend*), perfectly aligning with *\"not yet decided\"*.\n  - **Option D (\"didn't\"):** Simple past tense negative auxiliary (*did not*), which creates a tense clash with the future time marker *\"this evening\"*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is **Option C (might)**.",
